title: Genesis 4 – Cain and Abel
description: A verse-by-verse study of Genesis 4: two offerings, the first murder, the line of Cain, and the line of Seth, where men began to call upon the name of the LORD.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-4-cain-and-abel.html

=== Introduction
--- Cain and Abel [title]
overline: Young Adult Sunday School • Genesis Series
heading: Genesis 4: Cain and Abel
sub: The First Murder • Calling on the {Lord}
refs: Genesis 4:1-26
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== Two brothers
--- Two Sons Born
kicker: vv. 1–2
sub: Cain and Abel
scripture: Genesis 4:1-2
p: Eve’s declaration shows her faith that the promised seed of [[Genesis 3:15]] would come from the {Lord}. Two brothers, two vocations, and two very different hearts before God.

--- Two Offerings: One Accepted, One Rejected
kicker: vv. 3–5
sub: Abel’s Offering Accepted, Cain’s Rejected
scripture: Genesis 4:3-5
p: Abel brought blood, the firstlings of the flock. Cain brought the fruit of his own labor from the cursed ground. God had already shown in [[Genesis 3:21]] that a blood covering was required. [[Hebrews 9:22|Heb. 9:22]] The difference was not the quality of the gift but the nature of it, and the faith behind it.

--- God Warns Cain
kicker: vv. 6–7
sub: “Sin Lieth at the Door”: Grace Before Judgment
scripture: Genesis 4:6-7
p: God gives Cain a direct warning before he acts. Sin is pictured as a crouching beast at the threshold, desiring to master him, but he can still rule over it. Cain chose not to. God never sends judgment without first sending warning. [[1 Peter 5:8|1 Pet. 5:8]]

=== The first murder
--- Cain Rises Against Abel
kicker: v. 8
sub: The World’s First Murder: Brother Against Brother
scripture: Genesis 4:8
p: The first death in human history is not natural. It is murder. And the victim is a righteous man killed by a religious man whose worship was rejected. [[Matthew 23:35|Matt. 23:35]]: Jesus names Abel as the first of a long line of righteous martyrs.

--- God’s Judgment: The Blood Cries Out
kicker: vv. 9–12
sub: “Am I My Brother’s Keeper?” The Voice of Blood
scripture: Genesis 4:9-12
p: Cain lies to God as Adam hid from God. But the blood itself is a witness. [[Hebrews 12:24|Heb. 12:24]]: the blood of Jesus speaketh better things than that of Abel. Abel’s blood cried for vengeance; Christ’s blood speaks peace.

--- Hebrews 11:4: He Being Dead Yet Speaketh
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 4:10
fig: Hebrews 11:4 | Hebrews 11:4 | The difference between the two offerings was faith. Abel’s sacrifice was accepted not because it was larger or costlier, but because it was offered in faith, faith that required blood, pointing to the coming atonement. Though murdered, Abel still speaks through his faith recorded in Scripture.
fig: Hebrews 12:24 | Hebrews 12:24 | Abel’s blood cried out from the ground for justice and vengeance. The blood of Jesus speaks better things: it cries for mercy, for peace, for forgiveness. Both bloods speak; only one silences the accusation of the Law.

--- Hebrews 11:4: He Being Dead Yet Speaketh (continued)
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 4:10
fig: Romans 3:25 | Romans 3:25 | Every Old Testament blood sacrifice pointed forward to the propitiation Christ would make. Abel’s faith looked forward to the same cross we look back on.
callout: Abel did not earn God’s favor by working harder than Cain. He came God’s way, by faith, with blood. The entire book of Hebrews is built on this pattern: the old sacrifices were shadows; Christ is the substance. Abel understood by faith what the Law would later codify and the cross would finally fulfill.

--- Cain’s Punishment: Departure from God’s Presence
kicker: vv. 13–16
sub: Cain Went Out from the Presence of the {Lord}
scripture: Genesis 4:13-16
p: Cain’s complaint is about his suffering, not about what he has done. He does not repent; he grieves his consequences. Note that even in judgment God shows restraint, setting a mark of protection on Cain. Yet the defining sentence of verse 16 is the saddest in the chapter: he went out from the presence of the {Lord}.

--- 1 John 3:12: Not as Cain
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 4:16
fig: 1 John 3:11-12 | 1 John 3:11-12 | John names the root of Cain’s murder: his works were evil and his brother’s righteous. Righteousness in another does not produce love in a corrupt heart; it produces hatred. This is the spirit that has always killed the prophets.
fig: 1 John 3:15 | 1 John 3:15 | John draws the line from Cain to the heart. Murder did not begin in the field; it began with the hatred before it.

--- 1 John 3:12: Not as Cain (continued)
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 4:16
fig: John 8:44 | John 8:44 | The spirit behind Cain’s act is identified: the devil was a murderer from the beginning. Cain, John says in [[1 John 3:12]], “was of that wicked one.”
callout: Cain went out from the presence of the {Lord}, the saddest verse of the chapter. His legacy is not only the first murder but a pattern John warns the church against: religious performance without genuine faith, and hatred of the righteous because their righteousness is a rebuke to our own evil works.

=== The line of Cain
--- Civilization Without God
kicker: vv. 17–22
sub: Cain’s Descendants: City, Culture, Craft. Progress Without God: Building, Music, Metalwork
scripture: Genesis 4:17-22
p: The line of Cain builds civilization: cities, livestock, music, metalwork. None of it is evil in itself. But all of it is constructed apart from God. Man can fill the earth with art and industry and still be walking away from the presence of the {Lord}.

--- Lamech’s Boast: Violence Escalates
kicker: vv. 23–24
sub: Lamech: The Way of Cain Goes Further
scripture: Genesis 4:23-24
p: Lamech takes God’s promise of protection over Cain and twists it into a boast of personal vengeance. He has murdered and glories in it. This is sin unchecked, from Cain’s silent deed in a field to Lamech’s proud song. [[Romans 1:32|Rom. 1:32]]

--- Jude 11: The Way of Cain
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 4:24
fig: Jude 10-11 | Jude 1:10-11 | Jude uses “the way of Cain” as a defined category of apostasy: approaching God on your own terms, rejecting the blood, hating the righteous, and ultimately being driven from God’s presence. Lamech’s boast in verse 24 is the fruit of what Cain’s way produces across generations.
fig: Compare: Matthew 18:21-22 | Matthew 18:21-22 | Jesus directly inverts Lamech’s boast. Lamech: avenge seventy and sevenfold. Christ: forgive seventy times seven. The way of Cain multiplies vengeance; the way of Christ multiplies forgiveness.
callout: The way of Cain is the pattern of all religion that approaches God on its own terms, scorns the blood, hates the righteousness of others, and is driven further from God’s presence. Jude warns the church that this spirit appears among false teachers in every age.

=== The line of Seth
--- Seth Appointed: Men Call on the {Lord}
kicker: vv. 25–26
sub: “Then Began Men to Call upon the Name of the {Lord}”
scripture: Genesis 4:25-26 | mark: then began men to call upon the name of the LORD
p: Against the backdrop of Cain’s city and Lamech’s boast, God quietly raises up another seed. Seth, appointed in Abel’s place, begins the godly line through which the promised Redeemer will come. And the mark of that line is this: they call upon the name of the {Lord}.

--- Romans 10:13: Whosoever Shall Call
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 4:26
fig: Romans 10:13 | Romans 10:13 | What began at Seth’s generation, calling upon the name of the Lord, is still the only way of salvation. The promise in Genesis 4:26 finds its fullest declaration here in Paul’s letter to Rome.
fig: Romans 10:9-10 | Romans 10:9-10 | Calling upon the name of the Lord flows from a heart that believes and a mouth that confesses. The same faith Abel had, Seth’s generation expressed publicly in calling on the {Lord}.

--- Joel 2:32 and Acts 2:21
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 4:26
fig: Joel 2:32 | Joel 2:32 | This promise spans both Testaments: Joel, Romans 10:13, and Acts 2:21 all repeat it. What Seth’s generation began, the Church continues.
fig: Acts 2:21 | Acts 2:21 | Peter quotes Joel on the Day of Pentecost. The same promise first glimpsed in Genesis 4:26 is now proclaimed to all nations. The door has always been the same: call upon the {Lord}.

--- Call upon the {Lord}
center: Genesis 4 ends not with Lamech’s boast but with a quiet, eternal answer: men began to call upon the name of the {Lord}. Against every city Cain built and every song Lamech sang of blood, God was raising up a people who would simply call on Him. That calling, by faith, upon His name, is still the only way of salvation today.
